[38;5;231m⏺[39m There's a wrinkle worth surfacing: [1mthe app has no settings today.[0m No theme, no toggles, no options — just a login
  form. So "persist settings across sessions" has nothing to persist yet, and the task's real shape depends on whether
  you want the storage layer alone or at least one setting actually using it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this task ends with a module nothing calls, or with something visibly working.

  - [1mStorage module only[0m — a [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m with get/set/remove/clear over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, plus tests. Buys a clean, well-tested
    foundation with no UI guesswork. Costs: nothing in the app calls it, so "settings persist" isn't demonstrably true
    yet — you'd be taking the persistence on faith until a later task adds a consumer.
  - [1mStorage module + one real setting wired end-to-end[0m — the same module, plus one genuine preference in the UI so you
    can reload the page and see it hold. The obvious candidate is a "Remember my username" checkbox on the login form:
    it's real, it's tied to the flow that already exists, and it needs no new screens. Buys a working, observable
    feature and proves the module's shape against a real consumer. Costs: a small amount of UI in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m/[38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
  - [1mStorage module + a settings panel[0m — the module plus a dedicated settings UI with a few options (theme, etc.). Buys
    the full feature. Costs: the options would be invented by me rather than driven by a need, and a theme toggle on a
    page with no styling is busywork. This is the YAGNI risk.

  [1mMy recommendation: the module plus one real setting.[0m It's the smallest version that makes the headline claim actually
  true, and designing the interface against one real caller is what keeps it from being wrong. On "remember username"
  specifically — a username is not a credential and storing it is standard, but it is mildly identifying on a shared
  machine, so it'd be opt-in via an unchecked-by-default box, with the password never touched.
